Domain Model
Core entities and their relationships:
Model: top-level entity with a name, description, and audit timestamps. A model has many Versions.
Version: a specific iteration of a model, with framework, algorithm, artifact URI, training data reference, lifecycle stage, and approval status. Versions follow a lifecycle: DRAFT -> VALIDATED -> APPROVED -> STAGING -> PRODUCTION -> ARCHIVED.
Deployment: a request to deploy a specific version to an environment. Deployments follow a state machine: REQUESTED -> DEPLOYING -> SUCCEEDED/FAILED -> ROLLED_BACK. Each deployment tracks an event history.
Key governance rules:
Only APPROVED versions can be promoted to STAGING or PRODUCTION
Only APPROVED versions can be deployed
Only FAILED deployments can be retried
Only SUCCEEDED deployments can be rolled back
Idempotency keys prevent duplicate deployments

Key Workflows
Model Registration and Approval
1.User creates a model (POST /models)
2.User registers a version (POST /models/{id}/versions) -> starts as DRAFT
3.User approves the version (POST /.../approve) -> moves to APPROVED
4.User promotes to PRODUCTION (POST /.../promote) -> governance check passes
Deployment with Idempotency
1.User requests deployment with an idempotency key (POST /deployments)
2.System checks: is the version approved? If not, reject with 409.
3.System checks: has this idempotency key been used? If yes, return original.
4.Deployment is created with status REQUESTED, event is logged.
5.On failure: user can retry (POST /deployments/{id}/retry) -> only if FAILED
6.On bad deployment: user can rollback (POST /deployments/{id}/rollback) -> only if SUCCEEDED
Reliability
Idempotency: duplicate deployment requests with the same key return the original result, preventing accidental double-deploys
State validation: retry and rollback enforce valid state transitions, preventing unsafe operations (e.g., rolling back an already-failed deployment)
Input validation: Pydantic models reject malformed requests at the API boundary before they reach business logic
Error handling: consistent HTTP error codes (404 for not found, 409 for state conflicts) with clear messages
Future reliability improvements
Database transactions for atomic operations
Optimistic locking for concurrent access
Circuit breakers for external service calls
Dead letter queues for failed async operations




