Scaling
Current design limitations
In-memory storage: data lost on restart, single-process only
Synchronous processing: long-running operations block the API
Scaling approach for thousands of models
Persistence: migrate to PostgreSQL with proper indexing on model_id, version_id, and deployment status for efficient queries at scale
Async processing: use Celery with Redis/RabbitMQ for deployment operations, allowing the API to return immediately while deployments run in background workers
Caching: Redis cache for frequently accessed model metadata, reducing database load for read-heavy inventory views
Database partitioning: partition deployment and metrics tables by time for efficient querying of recent data while retaining history
Horizontal scaling: stateless API servers behind a load balancer, with shared database and cache, allowing independent scaling of API and worker tiers
Pagination: API endpoints would support cursor-based pagination for large model and deployment lists
Trade-offs

Decision	Chosen	Alternative	Rationale
Storage	In-memory dict	SQLAlchemy + PostgreSQL	Faster to implement for vertical slice; persistence is the clear next step
Frontend	Plain HTML/JS	Angular + TypeScript	Achievable in time constraints; proves frontend-backend integration works
Sync vs Async	Synchronous	Celery + message queue	Simpler for the current scope; async needed for real deployment operations
Idempotency	Key-based in-memory lookup	Database-backed with TTL	Demonstrates the pattern; production needs persistent storage and key expiry
Auth	None (open)	OAuth2 + RBAC	Out of scope for vertical slice; noted as a must-have for production
Deployment state	Simple status field	Full state machine with transition log	Current approach validates transitions; production would use a formal state machine pattern
